# Runtime M unrolling (rvv::matmul_t's m_block)
#
# N takes its accumulators first; an unbounded M unrolls over the vector
# groups left, up to 4 tiles, exactly as a large literal does. A narrow N at
# a small LMUL leaves those groups, so M runs its main stage and every tail
# stage of {4, 2, 1}, then a partial last vector; the runtime and literal
# kernels must agree on all of them.
--reset
--lmul=1,2
--shape=literal,runtime
1x1x9_n"one_element"
v-1x1x9_n"below_one_tile"
4vx1x9_n"one_main_stage"
4v+1x2x9_n"main_and_partial"
5vx1x9_n"main_and_one"
6v+3x2x9_n"main_two_and_partial"
7vx3x9_n"main_two_and_one"
11v+5x1x33_n"main_twice_and_tails"

# The tails under runtime strides, K below the unroll and the epilogue,
# which reads the M offset of every tile
--reset
--lmul=1
--shape=runtime --ld=runtime --kshape=literal,runtime
7v+5x2x3_n"short_k"
--post=alpha+beta+bias --alpha=2 --beta=0.5
7v+5x2x17_n"epilogue"

# Transposed operands keep their own M stride
--reset
--lmul=2
--shape=literal,runtime --transA=0,1 --transB=0,1
6v+3x2x9_n"transposed"
